// Every string the player reads, in the language the game is written in.
//
// The keys are the English text itself and each row maps it to itself, which looks
// redundant and is not: this file is the index of what there is to translate. A lookup that
// misses falls through to the current language, then to English, then to the key, so the
// interface would work with this file empty - it was three rows long, and the cost was that
// `Loc.MissingKeys`, whose job is to be a translator's to-do list, compared every language
// against those three rows and always answered "nothing missing".
//
// To translate the game: copy this file to `localization/<language>/00_<language>.json`,
// translate the right-hand side of every row and leave the left alone. `language.name` is
// the one row where both sides are your own language's name for itself.
//
// Kept honest by the bench. `the English table indexes every string the game shows` fails
// if a caption in the source is missing from here, or if another language translates a key
// this file does not have - which is how a stale row or a mis-keyed one is caught. Captions
// assembled at runtime - a country's name, a number, a date - are not keys and are not
// expected here.

